test_ipcfuzz — IPC dispatch fallback fuzz
==========================================

Phase 0c chunk 7: pins the contract that every system server's
command dispatch has a panic-free fallback for unknown command
bytes. Sends a malformed message (cmd byte = 0xFE, which is
above every server's command-table maximum of 99) to each named
server's port and verifies the server replies promptly with the
-1 sentinel byte (0xFF).

Servers fuzzed: `disk`, `tfs`, `fs` (= VFS), `tty`, `pm`, `ds`,
`nic`, `inet`, `unix`. Each one ends its dispatch chain with an
`else` arm that builds a 1-byte reply containing `byte(-1)` and
calls `ipc_reply`. A regression that turned that arm into a
panic, an early `return` without reply, or a wrong reply length
would deadlock the caller — this test would then hang in CI
rather than print its `ok` line.

VFS is special: its `else` forwards unknown commands to the TFS
backend and relays the reply. With cmd=0xFE the backend (TFS)
replies status=-1 and `vfs_reply_len` collapses that to 1 byte,
so the surface is the same.

Test plan
---------

  1. For each server name, `port_lookup` → port. Skip if < 0
     (server not registered in this fixture).
  2. `ipc_send` a 1-byte buffer with `buf[0] = 0xFE`. Verify
     return is 0 and `reply[0]` is 0xFF.
  3. `ipc_send` a 32-byte buffer of all-0xFE. Verify the same.
     This catches dispatch paths that branch on `msg_len` before
     reading `buf[0]`.
  4. Print `ipcfuzz: ok`.

Pass criterion: prints `ipcfuzz: ok`. A hang (no output) means a
server's else branch stopped replying.

    module oskit.bin.test_ipcfuzz

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, port_lookup, ipc_send}


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fuzz_one(name: string)
        val port = port_lookup(name)
        if port < 0
            return

        var msg: [32]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 32
            msg[i] = byte(0xFE)
            i += 1
        var reply: [16]byte

The 1-byte send exercises the dispatch's minimum input. A
correct server reads `buf[0]` as the command byte, takes the
else branch, and replies `-1`.

        reply[0] = 0
        val r1 = ipc_send(port, msg, 1, reply, 16)
        if r1 != 0
            puts("ipcfuzz: bad ")
            puts(name)
            puts(": 1B ipc_send returned ")
            print_idec(r1)
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(1)
            return
        if (int(reply[0]) & 0xff) != 0xff
            puts("ipcfuzz: bad ")
            puts(name)
            puts(": 1B reply ")
            print_idec(int(reply[0]) & 0xff)
            puts(" not 0xff\n")
            exit_with(2)
            return

The 32-byte send exercises a longer payload of garbage. The
dispatch must still take the else branch off `buf[0]`, not get
confused by trailing bytes.

        reply[0] = 0
        val r2 = ipc_send(port, msg, 32, reply, 16)
        if r2 != 0
            puts("ipcfuzz: bad ")
            puts(name)
            puts(": 32B ipc_send returned ")
            print_idec(r2)
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(3)
            return
        if (int(reply[0]) & 0xff) != 0xff
            puts("ipcfuzz: bad ")
            puts(name)
            puts(": 32B reply ")
            print_idec(int(reply[0]) & 0xff)
            puts(" not 0xff\n")
            exit_with(4)
            return


    main(argv: []string)
        fuzz_one("disk")
        fuzz_one("tfs")
        fuzz_one("fs")
        fuzz_one("tty")
        fuzz_one("pm")
        fuzz_one("ds")
        fuzz_one("nic")
        fuzz_one("inet")
        fuzz_one("unix")
        puts("ipcfuzz: ok\n")
        exit()
